 \begin{abstract}
-Security controls for LLM agents typically validate evidence that is present, but they do not establish that task-required evidence was never silently removed before a decision. We study \emph{Selective Evidence Suppression} (SES), in which an adversary removes truthful members of a bounded evidence set without forging the evidence that remains or injecting a malicious instruction. We formalize \emph{Evidence Completeness Integrity} (ECI) and prove an observation-equivalence boundary: a verifier that receives only delivered evidence and metadata generated from that evidence cannot distinguish a genuinely complete set from a larger required set that was reduced before observation. \system{} realizes ECI as a trusted reference monitor that authenticates the expected source membership and blocks sensitive commits when required members are no longer structurally covered; the prototype uses a compositional evidence DAG, while a flat authenticated coverage certificate is sufficient when the complete source-ID set can be carried unchanged to the sink. On 49 public multi-file security-fixing commits, Qwen2.5-VL-7B was correct on 44/49 complete inputs (89.8\%), yet suppressing at most two changed files induced 20 additional errors across the full cohort (40.8\%). Qwen3 showed 26 additional attack-induced misses across the same 49 commits (53.1\%). Across Git and SOC structural traces, ECI rejected all 3,747 tested single-item suppressions with exact missing-ID attribution while admitting every complete trace, and multi-stage experiments preserved the same accountability through repeated aggregation. These results separate evidence authenticity from expected-set completeness: for bounded or commitment-capable sources, security-sensitive agent decisions require an independently established membership obligation before commit.
