 To separate semantic-payload duplication from coverage-representation cost, we measured three encodings over the 49 real commits. The raw textual patches occupied 168,766 UTF-8 bytes. A canonical flat list of source IDs occupied 6,217 bytes, while our deliberately minimal source-ID-plus-parent-edge representation occupied 12,630 bytes. Thus the compositional representation was about 2.03$\times$ the flat-ID manifest size while remaining 13.4$\times$ smaller than the raw patch payload. This is an encoding-footprint illustration rather than a memory benchmark: it excludes signatures, Python object overhead, and richer deployment metadata. The result should not be read as a space advantage over flat manifests; it quantifies the additional structural cost of the DAG representation.
 
