+\section{Data and Code Availability}
+The study uses two public-source datasets/artifacts: the TQRG security-patch dataset and the SecAlertBench public artifact, with frozen cohorts and cryptographic hashes recorded in the reproducibility package. The OmissionShield source code, frozen experiment configurations, per-case result artifacts, and scripts required to regenerate the reported non-LLM analyses are being assembled into a release package. No public archival repository URL or DOI has been assigned as of November 10, 2026; this manuscript therefore does not claim that a public code archive already exists. The current internal reproduction order is documented in \texttt{revision\_2026-11-10/REPRODUCE.md} and will be mirrored in the final archival package.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
-Selective Evidence Suppression exposes a gap between validating observed evidence and proving that a bounded evidence set was not adversarially reduced. We formalized that gap through observation equivalence, connected evidence deletion to decision-theoretic regret and robustness, introduced Evidence Completeness Integrity, and implemented \system{} as a trusted reference monitor with decision-time enforcement. The prototype uses a compositional DAG encoding of source coverage, while a flat authenticated coverage certificate remains a valid simpler representation when its full source-ID set can be carried unchanged to the sink. Frozen interventions on public multi-file security patches provide the primary empirical evidence that small targeted suppressions can reverse otherwise correct security judgments even when all remaining evidence is authentic; an SOC benchmark serves as a secondary stress test of the same structural threat under a different evidence schema. The same ECI mechanism deterministically detects structural loss across both settings and through multi-stage compression with low control-plane overhead. The guarantee is intentionally limited to bounded or commitment-capable sources and structural completeness. Within that scope, security-critical agent decisions should be able to prove not only where their evidence came from, but also that the evidence the task required did not silently disappear.
+Selective Evidence Suppression exposes a gap between validating observed evidence and proving that a bounded evidence set was not adversarially reduced. We formalized that gap through observation equivalence and Minimal Decisive Omission, introduced Evidence Completeness Integrity, and implemented \system{} as reference-monitor logic with decision-time enforcement under an explicit TCB. The prototype uses a compositional DAG encoding of source coverage, while a flat authenticated coverage certificate remains a valid simpler representation when its full source-ID set can be carried unchanged to the sink. Frozen interventions on public multi-file security patches provide the primary empirical evidence that small targeted suppressions can reverse otherwise correct security judgments even when all remaining evidence is authentic; an SOC benchmark serves as a secondary stress test of the same structural threat under a different evidence schema. The same ECI mechanism deterministically detects structural loss across both settings and through multi-stage compression with low control-plane overhead. The guarantee is intentionally limited to bounded or commitment-capable sources and structural completeness. Within that scope, security-critical agent decisions should be able to prove not only where their evidence came from, but also that the evidence the task required did not silently disappear.
 
